---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro'
import { LINKS, SITE } from '../../consts'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How this website, the Playarr Android app, the hosted web app, a self-hosted Playarr server and the public linking service handle data."
>
  <Section eyebrow="Legal" title="Privacy">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="legal-effective-date">Effective 28 August 2026</p>
      <p class="lede">
        Four separate things are worth keeping apart: this website, the server you run yourself,
        the Playarr apps, and the hosted Playarr service.
      </p>

      <h2>Who this policy covers</h2>
      <p>
        This policy applies to the Playarr Android application with package name
        <code>app.playarr.mobile</code>, the Playarr web application at
        <a href={LINKS.playarrApp}>playarr.app</a>, and the short-lived device-linking service on
        that domain. Playarr connects to a Playarr Server chosen and operated by you or your
        server administrator.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is a set of static pages. It sets no tracking cookies, runs no analytics, embeds
        no third-party scripts, and has no forms to submit. The single piece of state it keeps is
        your light or dark theme preference, stored in your own browser and never transmitted
        anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whoever hosts these pages will keep ordinary web-server request logs, as any web server
        does.
      </p>

      <h2>The server you run</h2>
      <p>
        A {SITE.name} server is yours. Your library, your accounts, your watch history and your
        artwork cache live on your own storage, under your own control. The project operates no
        service that this data is sent to.
      </p>
      <p>
        The server does reach out to the library-management applications and metadata sources you
        configure it to reach, that is the point of configuring them, and it fetches artwork from
        the URLs those applications provide, caching it locally.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sign-in details go directly to your selected Playarr Server. The server may hold account
        and profile details, library metadata, artwork, watch history, playlists, download and
        playback state, server addresses, device identifiers, sessions, invitations and push
        notification registrations. The person or organisation operating that server controls this
        data, its backups, retention and deletion. Playarr's hosted service does not receive your
        password, library, watch history or media files.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ask your server administrator to correct or delete server-side account data. Server
        administrators can also revoke sessions and remove push registrations.
      </p>

      <h2>The Playarr apps</h2>
      <p>
        This covers the Playarr apps for iPhone, iPad, Apple TV, Android, Android TV and the web.
        The developer collects no data from them. The apps contain no advertising, no tracking, and
        no third-party analytics or crash-reporting libraries, and they do not follow you across
        other companies' apps or websites.
      </p>
      <p>What the apps keep on your own device:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the addresses of the servers you add,</li>
        <li>
          your sign-in session, held in the operating system's secure storage (the Keychain on
          Apple devices),
        </li>
        <li>your preferences, such as language, theme and playback settings,</li>
        <li>
          anything you choose to download for offline viewing, with its playback progress, which
          is removed when you delete the download or the app,
        </li>
        <li>
          a random per-install device identifier, and authentication and refresh tokens.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Everything else the apps show, including your library, watch history and accounts, is
        fetched from your own server and handled as described under "The server you run". Signing
        out, or deleting the app, removes the session and settings from the device. Deleting your
        account or history is a matter for whoever runs your server.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Apple devices the app asks for local network access so that it can find your server and
        any Chromecast devices on your home network. Chromecast support uses Google's Cast
        software, which is governed by Google's own privacy policy. Apple may separately give the
        developer aggregate, non-identifying crash and usage reports if you have chosen to share
        them in your device settings. Beta testers on TestFlight are also subject to Apple's
        TestFlight terms.
      </p>

      <h2>Notifications and Google services</h2>
      <p>
        In production Android builds configured for invite notifications, Google Firebase Cloud
        Messaging creates an app-installation identifier and push token after you sign in. The
        token is registered with your selected Playarr Server so it can deliver invitation
        notifications; notification permission controls whether Android displays them. Playarr
        does not use Firebase Analytics, advertising SDKs or cross-app tracking. Google's handling
        of Firebase data is governed by its own terms and privacy policy.
      </p>

      <h2>The hosted web app</h2>
      <p>
        playarr.app does not use advertising cookies, analytics pixels or behavioural profiling.
        The signed-in web app can store server addresses, device identifiers, names, access and
        refresh tokens, interface preferences, download records and media, and playback progress
        waiting to sync in your browser. Hosting and network providers may process ordinary
        request information such as an IP address, timestamp, requested path and user agent for
        delivery, reliability and abuse prevention. Playarr does not sell personal data.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you sign in to the hosted web client, playarr.app sets a <code>playarr_signed_in</code>
        cookie holding only <code>1</code>. It is used only to decide whether the home page shows
        the app or this site. It is strictly necessary, is not used for tracking, and is removed
        when you sign out.
      </p>

      <h2>The hosted Playarr service</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={LINKS.playarrApp}>playarr.app</a> hosts the Playarr web application
        and can broker the first handshake between a new app and a server it has not met. That is
        the one moment something passes through infrastructure the project operates.
      </p>
      <p>The short-lived link record contains only:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the device secret generated by the app,</li>
        <li>the server addresses you approve,</li>
        <li>the client platform, and</li>
        <li>a single-use device code.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        It never contains a password, library data, a bearer token, or a refresh token. The record
        expires after five minutes, with only a brief redemption grace period, and is then
        deleted. After linking, the app talks to your server directly and nothing further
        routes through the hosted service.
      </p>

      <h2>Security, retention and deletion</h2>
      <p>
        The hosted playarr.app service and the Google Play Android app require HTTPS. Sideloaded
        builds can also connect to an HTTP address chosen by you or your administrator, so only
        use an unencrypted address on a network you trust. Local records remain until removed in
        the app, cleared by the operating system or removed by uninstalling. Server-side records
        and backups follow the server operator's retention policy. Device-link records expire
        automatically as described above.
      </p>
      <p>
        To request deletion of an account and associated server-side data, follow the
        <a href="/legal/account-deletion">account-deletion instructions</a>. Signing out does not
        itself delete a server account or all local records.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        You can avoid the hosted linking service by entering a server address and signing in
        directly. You can disable notifications, remove downloads, sign out, clear app storage or
        uninstall the app at any time.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If any of the above changes, particularly if analytics are ever added to this site, or the
        hosted service ever holds more than it does today, this page will be updated to say so
        before the change ships. The effective date above identifies the current version.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy, or requests about data held by the hosted service, can go to
        <a href="mailto:support@playarr.app">support@playarr.app</a>. Playarr is developed by
        Thomas McFarlane. You can also use the contact method on the
        <a href={LINKS.repo} rel="noopener">Playarr repository</a>. Do not put passwords, tokens
        or private information in a public issue.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
  }

  .legal-effective-date {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .prose ul {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-inline-start: 1.35em;
  }

  .prose li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
</style>
